\begin{proof}
Let $g\in G$. Since $K\trianglelefteq G$, conjugation by $g$ restricts to an automorphism of $K$. Because $H$ is characteristic in $K$, this automorphism maps $H$ to itself. Thus $gHg^{-1}=H$ for every $g\in G$, so $H\trianglelefteq G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
To prove that $H$ is normal in $G$, let $g\in G$. Define $\varphi_g:K\to K$ by $\varphi_g(k)=gkg^{-1}$. This is well-defined because $K\trianglelefteq G$, and it is an automorphism of $K$. Since $H$ is characteristic in $K$, every automorphism of $K$ preserves $H$, so $\varphi_g(H)=H$. Therefore $gHg^{-1}=H$ for every $g\in G$. Hence $H\trianglelefteq G$.
\end{proof}
